        "## Key Hypotheses About Drivers Who Accept Bar Coupons\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Habit & Lifestyle Alignment is the Dominant Factor\n",
        "* **Hypothesis:** Drivers accept coupons for venues that match their existing routines and social habits; coupons rarely induce new or unfamiliar lifestyle behaviors.\n",
        "* **Evidence:** Drivers who go to a bar more than once a month accept bar coupons at **68.79%**, compared to just **29.35%** for non-goers or rare visitors ($\\le 1$ time per month). When visit frequency increases to more than 3 times a month, acceptance leaps to **76.88%**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Youth and Social Setting Lower Friction to Accept\n",
        "* **Hypothesis:** Younger drivers (under 30) and those traveling with social peers (friends/partners) have higher spontaneous flexibility and lower friction to accept a bar detour.\n",
        "* **Evidence:**\n",
        "  * Drivers under the age of 30 accept bar coupons at **48.94%**, compared to **34.62%** for drivers 30 and older.\n",
        "  * Drivers accompanied by friends accept at **55.79%**, whereas drivers accompanied by kids accept at a minimal **20.87%**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Contextual Appropriateness Suppresses or Enables Action\n",
        "* **Hypothesis:** The presence of dependents (children) or conflicting commitments heavily suppresses bar coupon conversion, regardless of demographic or financial background.\n",
        "* **Evidence:** Removing child passengers from frequent bar-goers pushes the acceptance rate up to **71.32%**, whereas drivers with children accept bar coupons at a rate lower than half the general population average.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Income & General Discount Sensitivity Are Secondary\n",
        "* **Hypothesis:** General deal sensitivity (e.g., using coupons at cheap restaurants or having lower income) does not translate into bar coupon acceptance unless paired with an existing bar habit.\n",
        "* **Evidence:** Frequent cheap restaurant goers with income under $50K accepted bar coupons at only **45.35%**, whereas targeting frequent bar-goers directly yielded **>70%** conversion regardless of income.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Executive Summary for Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Core Profile of a High-Converting Bar Coupon Target:**\n",
        "> A driver under age 30 who already visits bars at least 1–3 times a month, driving alone or with adult friends, without children present in the vehicle."
